// EDIT ME: your Michelin collection, any order (the page sorts by stars, then newest first).
// name, city = "City, ST" for the US and Canada (two-letter state or province), otherwise "City, Country"   date = "YYYY-MM-DD", "YYYY-MM" or "YYYY" (optional)  keepOrder = true to show the date but leave the card where it is in the grid  cover = square thumbnail for the card (optional)  photos = ["file.jpg", ...] in assets/photos/  body = ["paragraph", { "photo": "file.jpg" }, ...] (a photo object places it between paragraphs)
// stars = 1, 2 or 3   bib = true for a Bib Gourmand   link and note are optional.
// recommended = true for a restaurant in the Michelin Guide without stars (shows the Michelin logo).
// A restaurant with none of these shows no mark.
// body_zh / body_ja / body_ko = the same as body, in Chinese / Japanese / Korean (all optional). A restaurant that has any gets a switch on its page (H 何 か 하, only the languages it has); one with none shows no switch. Keep small place names in English or the local language.
window.PLATES = [
  {"name": "Quince", "city": "San Francisco, CA", "stars": 3, "date": "2026-10-10", "cover": "quince_cover.jpg", "body": ["Quince is Michael Tusk's San Francisco restaurant, a three-star kitchen where Italian training and French technique meet whatever is best in the California season. I came on my birthday, with my parents, the first birthday the three of us had spent together since 2007, so I was paying close attention to the food and to who was sitting across from me.", {"photo": "quince_1.jpeg"}, "The menu card was waiting at my place with a pressed-flower print and a handwritten line, “Happy Birthday, Haotian!”, next to a porcelain plate embossed with tiny blossoms. A coupe of amber-orange juice and a vase of pink flowers finished a table that felt more like a good home than a temple of gastronomy.", "The first plate showed the whole approach. Artichoke, sliced so thin and translucent that it looked like shaved garlic, was fanned over a toasted round beside a pale golden sauce and a few tiny white blossoms. Raw vegetables are easy to treat as a garnish. Here the artichoke was the dish.", {"photo": "quince_3.jpeg"}, "Next came a scalloped cappellacci in a bowl of green herb oil and a light foam, with a single leaf on top. The pasta had been filled with sweet corn, and when I bit through the skin it released the juice of an October cornfield. It was an autumn flavor delivered in one precise bite.", {"photo": "quince_4.jpeg"}, "The hand-cut tagliatelle was the richest course: ribbons of golden egg pasta, coated in a pigeon ragù, with California golden chanterelles from October and a drift of shaved cheese. It was a classical Italian idea cooked with real depth, and the mushrooms tied it to the place and the month.", {"photo": "quince_2.jpeg"}, "The meal ended its savory run with a French classic, a roasted spring chicken. The skin was glossy and lacquered, the breast pink and moist, the leg crisped in a crumb crust, and the plate was finished with a dark jus, braised greens, small potatoes, glazed onions and mushrooms. A brioche roll and a quenelle of butter sat on the side. It was a conservative dish, and the confidence to end on it says a lot.", {"photo": "quince_5.jpeg"}, "What I admire most about Quince is that October runs through everything. The produce, the pasta fillings and the mushrooms all taste of the same month, and the Italian and French training shows up in every detail without ever showing off. I also love the concept of chef-owned farms. When the person who writes the menu also decides what gets grown, the cooking starts in the soil, and you can taste that intention on the plate. The result is calm and restrained, the work of an experienced hand.", {"photo": "quince_6.jpeg"}]},
  {"name": "Ikoyi", "city": "London, United Kingdom", "stars": 2, "date": "2026-07-23", "cover": "ikoyi_cover.jpg", "body": ["Ikoyi is Jeremy Chan's restaurant in St James's, a two-star kitchen built on spices, which the printed note on the menu describes as a cuisine of slowly grown vegetables, line-caught fish and aged native breed beef. I had only an hour and a half free at lunch, not enough for even the short tasting, so I asked for a custom mini tasting menu, and the kitchen kindly made one.", {"photo": "ikoyi_1.jpeg"}, "The dry-ageing cabinets by the dining room set the tone: whole fish fillets and mackerel hanging on hooks, large cuts of beef, and trays of pigeon. It is a kitchen that spends time on the ingredient before any spice goes near it. I also noticed, only while sitting there, that the Ikoyi logo is an axially symmetric figure.", {"photo": "ikoyi_2.jpeg"}, "The menu card was dated 23rd July 2026, with a letter of thanks and a signature. Its dishes read: Gola Pepper Broth; Drunken Squid Toast; Turbot, Crab Salad & Cauliflower; Native Breed Rib & Bread Satay; Smoked Jollof Rice; Curry Leaf, Caramelised Poppy Seed & Cinnamon Berry; Pinecone Cookie; Melon & Rose; White Peach & Plum.", {"photo": "ikoyi_3.jpeg"}, "The fish course was a crisp-skinned piece under a veil of foam, with a mound of crab salad at one side, a bright orange sphere and a pool of green oil. The aroma arrived before the plate did.", {"photo": "ikoyi_4.jpeg"}, "The beef was the dish I remember best: dark, lacquered and glossy, with a smooth brown purée, a wild green herb on top, and a leaf-wrapped morsel on the side. The spice here has the warmth of North African cooking, and it was striking.", {"photo": "ikoyi_5.jpeg"}, "Dessert was Melon & Rose, a pink-dusted dome over a pale green base, sitting inside a rough stoneware bowl. Another sweet followed under shards of dark chocolate and a long lime-green leaf.", {"photo": "ikoyi_6.jpeg"}, "I was in a hurry, and a rushed lunch is not the way to judge a tasting menu. But the fragrance and the North African spicing were as good as I had hoped, and the cooking was impressive in those moments. Overall I rate it slightly above average."]},
  {"name": "Protégé", "city": "Palo Alto, CA", "stars": 1, "date": "2026-11-10", "cover": "protege_cover.jpg", "body": ["Protégé is a one-Michelin-star restaurant in Palo Alto. I went for dinner on November 10, 2026. Its reputation and the praise it gets made me expect a great deal, and I left feeling it was good but not as good as its fame.", {"photo": "protege_1.jpeg"}, "The meal opened with a showpiece: a brown eggshell filled with Dungeness crab and topped with a spoonful of caviar, set in a porcelain cup shaped like a duck's foot. It is playful and well made, and it announced a kitchen that cares about presentation.", {"photo": "protege_2.jpeg"}, "A seared octopus tentacle came next, blistered and golden at the edges, curled over artichoke in an olive and herb sauce, with a bright red pepper sauce, a cloud of foam and a few arugula leaves. The plate was carefully built, and the charred crust on the octopus was the best part of how it looked.", {"photo": "protege_3.jpeg"}, "The main course was A5 Miyazaki wagyu, cut into a thick block and served with fried mushrooms, a glossy brown sauce, a dark purée and a nasturtium leaf. A5 is the highest grade of Japanese beef, and Miyazaki is one of the most celebrated regions for it. It is also extremely rich. Two pieces of it left me heavy for a long time afterward. I am getting older, and I should stop showing off with rich food.", {"photo": "protege_4.jpeg"}, "I rate Protégé a little below average, or at best average, for a restaurant with its fame and public praise. The plates were polished and the presentation was memorable, but the meal did not match what the reputation had led me to expect."]},
  {"name": "Sushi Shin", "city": "Redwood City, CA", "date": "2026-11-11", "cover": "sushishin_cover.jpg", "body": ["Sushi Shin is a sushi counter in Redwood City, led by Chef Zhan. It was a Michelin one-star restaurant but has not held the star since 2024. After eating at Wa Shin in Boston, another counter run by a Chinese chef, I wanted to see how a second Chinese-led sushi restaurant would approach the Edomae tradition.", {"photo": "sushishin_01_buri-toro.jpeg"}, "The meal began with buri (adult Japanese amberjack, or yellowtail) from Chiba Prefecture, in the fatty belly cut known as buri-toro. It was a thick, pale-pink slice, finely veined, with a mound of grated daikon and a tangle of scallion on a textured glass plate. Even the chopsticks carried the restaurant's mark.", {"photo": "sushishin_02_chef-grilling-fish.jpeg"}, "At the counter, Chef Zhan worked on small pieces of white fish that had been skewered and grilled, and he plated them on small blue-striped dishes in front of each guest.", {"photo": "sushishin_03_grilled-fish-nigiri.jpeg"}, "The grilled fish came as nigiri, skin side up, its scored, charred skin shining on the rice.", {"photo": "sushishin_04_monkfish-handroll.jpeg"}, "A hand roll followed, a nori cone held in the hand, filled with monkfish prepared with ginger and sweet soy sauce, over a small amount of rice. The filling was soft and creamy.", {"photo": "sushishin_05_shishamo.jpeg"}, "The dish that best showed Chef Zhan's personal logic of umami was a whole Hokkaido shishamo (capelin), cooked in the style of Shanghai smoked fish (熏鱼). The skin was lacquered a deep, sticky brown and sprinkled with toasted sesame, and the fish lay on a hand-painted yellow plate. A Shanghai home-style flavor applied to a Japanese fish is not something the Edomae mainstream would do, and Chef Zhan seems more willing than most to do it.", {"photo": "sushishin_06_mapo-shirako.jpeg"}, "The second was Hokkaido cod milt (shirako) in a mapo-style sauce built on Pixian doubanjiang, dusted with chili powder and served on a floral plate. Sichuan heat laid over one of the most delicate ingredients in the Japanese kitchen is again very much his own idea.", {"photo": "sushishin_07_sayori.jpeg"}, "Winter has arrived, so the Hokkaido bafun uni of the warm months is gone, and the Kansai kijihata (a grouper) was not available. The kitchen replaced them with what was close by: purple sea urchin from Santa Barbara, and needlefish (sayori) from the Seto Inland Sea. The needlefish nigiri, with its thin silver line running down the fish, was a clean, handsome piece.", {"photo": "sushishin_08_toro.jpeg"}, "The fatty tuna (toro) was North Atlantic bluefin, cut in the hagashi style to make it more tender. It was a deep, glossy red with fine marbling, and its layered surface showed the cut.", {"photo": "sushishin_09_saba.jpeg"}, "Saba (mackerel) followed, a skin-on nigiri with the blue-silver skin laid over golden flesh.", {"photo": "sushishin_10_uni.jpeg"}, "The Santa Barbara uni came as a nori-wrapped gunkan, heaped high with orange tongues of urchin that spilled over the sides and shone under the counter lights.", {"photo": "sushishin_11_anago.jpeg"}, "Anago, the saltwater conger eel and a different fish from freshwater unagi, came as a simmered, glazed piece on a gold-ringed glass plate.", {"photo": "sushishin_12_tuna-handroll.jpeg"}, "The savory courses ended with a tall hand roll of minced tuna, held upright in a cone of nori while the chef prepared the next.", {"photo": "sushishin_13_sesame-tofu.jpeg"}, "Dessert was sesame tofu, soft and pale, sitting in a pool of dark syrup and served with a small gold spoon.", "I rate Sushi Shin average, for a restaurant that once held a Michelin star. For a Chinese diner it is a real pleasure: the flavors are familiar, and there is a fresh charm in seeing them on sushi, especially in the shishamo and the mapo shirako. But judged as Edomae sushi itself, the balance between rice and fish, the overall tone, and the harmony of iki (粋) still have room to grow."]},
  {"name": "Pagu", "city": "Cambridge, MA", "recommended": true, "date": "2026-08-17", "cover": "pagu_cover.jpg", "body": ["Pagu is a small Cambridge restaurant in Central Square known for blending Japanese and Spanish cooking, and it is listed in the Michelin Guide as a Recommended restaurant. I went on August 17, 2026, with high hopes, and left feeling it did not reach them.", {"photo": "pagu_1.jpeg"}, "The first plate was a raw fish crudo: thick slices of pale fish, which looked like yellowtail, in a citrus-and-soy dressing with ground pepper, shredded radish, purple shiso leaves and orange marigold petals. It was pretty and the fish was fresh, but the dressing was thin and the flavors did not come together into anything I would remember.", {"photo": "pagu_2.jpeg"}, "Next came the anchovies: a fan of white anchovy fillets in a pool of green olive oil, dusted with a seasoning of chili and sesame in the Japanese style, with bread alongside. The idea, Spanish boquerones with a Japanese spice, is clever and well suited to the restaurant. The fish was mild and clean, though the seasoning mostly sat on top, and it was a dish I would call good but ordinary.", "I rate Pagu below average. Neither dish was bad, and both showed the restaurant's concept, but they did not live up to my expectations."]},
  {"name": "Restaurant De Kas", "city": "Amsterdam, Netherlands", "stars": 1, "date": "2026-08-01", "cover": "dekas_cover.jpg", "body": ["Restaurant De Kas is often called the grandfather of farm-to-table dining, and it has been cooking that way for almost twenty-five years. It sits in Amsterdam in what used to be the city's municipal greenhouse, built in 1927. Today the front of the glasshouse is the dining room and the back is the vegetable garden. The idea is simple and strict: whatever comes out of the ground that day is what is on the plate. I went on August 1, 2026, at the height of tomato season, and I had wanted to come for a long time.", {"photo": "dekas_1.jpeg"}, "The meal began with a tall glass of sparkling drink with a single pink petal floating in it, and two small plates on the dark metal ware the restaurant uses. One held a curl of mustard leaf with a bright yellow sauce, a dusting of seeds and tiny purple shoots, and the other held two cones of shiso, rolled around a filling. The kitchen also ferments its surplus flowers and herbs into its own kombucha, so nothing that grows in the garden goes to waste.", {"photo": "dekas_2.jpeg"}, "An oyster shell on crushed ice came next, filled with a chopped red tartare, round green beads of purée, purple micro leaves and a white flower. It was colorful and pretty, and a good example of how the garden is used as a paint box.", {"photo": "dekas_3.jpeg"}, "What made the visit memorable was the greenhouse behind the dining room. Tall tomato plants climb on strings under the glass roof, with ripe fruit in red and near-black, beside beds of herbs and orange marigolds. I watched a cook walk out to the back garden, pick a tomato and bite into it on the spot. That was when I understood that the flowers and vegetables on my plate might have been in the sun, behind me, a few minutes earlier.", {"photo": "dekas_4.jpeg"}, "A bowl of beans, sliced vegetables and a green oil was covered with nasturtium leaves and flowers in orange and yellow, on a ribbed glass dish. It looked like a small garden, and the leaves had a peppery bite, but the cooking underneath was gentle and plain.", {"photo": "dekas_5.jpeg"}, "Mussels came in a light, golden foam, with broad-bean slices, coriander leaves, cornflowers, chive blossoms and sunflower petals piled on top, in a dark bowl. It was one of the most beautiful plates of the meal, and my favorite photograph of the day.", {"photo": "dekas_6.jpeg"}, "A later dish was an autumn-colored plate of roasted root, hazelnuts, apple batons and a pale sauce, buried under shavings of black truffle and purple oxalis leaves. The truffle is the one thing here that obviously did not come from the garden.", "I rate De Kas a little below average. I admire the concept and the discipline behind it, and seeing the tomatoes growing a few steps from my table was worth the trip. But the meal itself did not live up to the idea: the freshness of the produce was clear, but I wanted more from the cooking than the garden alone could give. It is a restaurant I am glad to have visited, and one whose story is stronger than its plates."]},
  {"name": "A. Wong", "city": "London, United Kingdom", "stars": 2, "date": "2026-07-24", "keepOrder": true, "cover": "awong_cover.jpg", "body": ["A. Wong is Andrew Wong's two-star restaurant in London, with a menu built around Cantonese dim sum. It was the Asian half of a day that I had planned as a double: Cantonese for lunch, Thai for dinner. I went in hoping for a kitchen that would use the classic dim sum vocabulary to say something new.", {"photo": "awong_1.jpeg"}, "The first steamer set the pattern. A siu mai was stacked with a crisp lace of fried skin and a glassy green leaf, and beside it a har gow was domed with a cloud of foam. Both are technically skillful and photograph beautifully, and both put more on the classic than the classic needed.", {"photo": "awong_2.jpeg"}, "A soup dumpling followed, closed at the top with a ring of pearl-like beads and a small cube topped with a ribbon of scallion. The wrapper is beautifully thin and the pleating is as clean as in the best dumpling houses, but the decoration feels like an afterthought rather than an idea.", {"photo": "awong_3.jpeg"}, "A triangular fried pastry arrived dusted all over with bright pink powder, as if the plate had been hit by a flash of paint.", {"photo": "awong_4.jpeg"}, "The most dramatic plate was a tall tower of spun, lacy golden threads crowned with a violet disc. It is a lot to look at, and it is hard to find a flavor in it that justified the construction.", {"photo": "awong_5.jpeg"}, "Then a small roll with caviar and shaved black truffle arrived on a banana leaf in a bamboo steamer, set in a wreath of dried grasses. The staging was spectacular, but it also said what the whole meal said: luxury and theatre have been added at every opportunity.", {"photo": "awong_6.jpeg"}, "A steamed bun shaped like a mandarin, with two glossy leaves for its stem, made me smile. It is a charming trick, and I would happily see more of it in the service of a clear point.", "My honest verdict is that A. Wong has plenty of showmanship and elaborate stacking, but it is short on a central idea. The dishes are busy and certainly skilled, yet the flavors did not stand out for me, and I left with a clear memory of how each plate looked and a much vaguer memory of how it tasted. I rate it not so good: the technique is there, but the point of view is thin."]},
  {"name": "ANAM", "city": "Seoul, Korea", "recommended": true, "date": "2025-06-18", "cover": "anam_cover.jpg", "body": ["ANAM is a Seoul restaurant that builds its menu around pork, and it is listed in the Michelin Guide among the Recommended restaurants. I went on June 18, 2025, and ordered the two dishes that tell you what the place is about: pork served two ways, as a sliced cold plate and as a hot rice soup. I rate it highly.", {"photo": "anam_1.jpeg"}, "The first plate was boiled pork, sliced thin and shingled on a white dish, with a dusting of coarse black pepper and chili over the fat, and a wedge of lime at its side. The meat was the pale pink of a gently poached cut, with a clean, white layer of fat on top, and it was served with a small bowl of cabbage kimchi and a bowl of fresh coriander. The pairing is simple and smart: the pepper and lime cut through the fat, the kimchi brings acidity and heat, and the coriander adds a bright, green note that you do not often find with Korean boiled pork. It was light, and it showed how clean the pork was.", {"photo": "anam_2.jpeg"}, "The second dish was a pork rice soup, in a large white bowl, with a clear, pale broth that was barely cloudy, rice under the surface, slices of tender lean pork, a rib on the bone, a mound of finely chopped scallion, more coriander and beads of bright green herb oil floating on top. A second empty bowl on the tray lets you divide the soup. The broth was clean and savory, with a restrained depth, and the herb oil and coriander lifted it away from the heavy, milky style that pork soup often has. The kimchi again cut through it.", "Both dishes make one point: ANAM treats pork with a light hand, using clear broth, careful poaching, and fresh herbs and citrus, and it gets a lot of flavor from few things. For a place this simple, the care is clear, and I left very satisfied."]},
  {"name": "Row on 5", "city": "London, United Kingdom", "stars": 2, "date": "2026-08-04", "cover": "rowon5_cover.jpg", "body": ["Row on 5 was the last dinner of this European trip, and it was a fitting way to finish. London's two-star dining room, carrying a 2026 Michelin plaque at its door, builds its tasting menu around what it calls a British Coastal Journey, a run of fourteen items that starts in a lounge downstairs and moves to the dining room upstairs. It is one of my rare favorites, and the meal was held together by an unusually precise kitchen.", {"photo": "rowon5_1.jpeg"}, {"photo": "rowon5_2.jpeg"}, "Getting in is part of the show: a polished brass sign, an olive tree in the window and a small plate by the door that reads “Press for Dinner”. Downstairs, a printed map of the British Isles explains the opening bites, each one tied to a coastline: Cornish bluefin tuna, Inverness langoustine from the Scottish Highlands, and a Louët-Feisser oyster from Carlingford Lough.", {"photo": "rowon5_3.jpeg"}, "The oyster was the dish of the night for me. It is visibly in conversation with Thomas Keller's Oysters and Pearls, with the same idea of a stacked composition: caviar set in a ring on a crisp base, and a perfectly glazed sphere on top. But I thought this version was the better one, even better than the original at The French Laundry. The finish is cleaner, the curve of the sphere is rounder, and it feels like the idea was sculpted again from the beginning. It is a rare case of the student overtaking the teacher.", {"photo": "rowon5_4.jpeg"}, "The other cold bites were just as polished, among them a small bowl nested in crushed ice and scattered with petals, served with a spoon on a porcelain cat rest, a small reminder that the kitchen has a sense of humor.", {"photo": "rowon5_5.jpeg"}, "Upstairs, the open kitchen runs at the pace of a studio. At the pass, the chefs plate with tweezers in white aprons, with no shouting and no wasted movement. The calm was visible, and it showed on every plate.", {"photo": "rowon5_6.jpeg"}, "The full menu reads: Cheese & Onion; Louët-Feisser oyster; Cornish bluefin tuna; Inverness langoustine in two parts; Orkney scallop; “Singapore” native lobster; John Dory au poivre; A5 Kagoshima wagyu; Colston Bassett Stilton; Miso & Truffle; wild Malaga strawberry; Tea & Cake; Oswaldo 71%; and petits fours. A printed card marked the evening as an anniversary.", {"photo": "rowon5_7.jpeg"}, "The Inverness langoustine does something clever: it is used twice. It closes the downstairs part of the meal and returns to open the upstairs one. In the second version, slices of the pink langoustine sit on green grains in a clear glass bowl, with a pale golden quenelle in a light broth. Seeing an ingredient come back in a new form gives the evening a thread.", {"photo": "rowon5_8.jpeg"}, "The Orkney scallop, from the north of Scotland, was cut into fine strands and served almost like a salad in a vivid green sauce, topped with slivers of red onion. It was delicate and light. The John Dory au poivre followed, a familiar North Sea fish I had last seen in May at KOMU in Munich.", {"photo": "rowon5_9.jpeg"}, "A bowl of silky custard crowned with caviar, sitting on a perforated porcelain tray, added a quieter, richer moment.", {"photo": "rowon5_10.jpeg"}, "The main course was A5 Kagoshima wagyu, grilled over open binchotan charcoal. The sauce is deep and dark: black truffle, shiitake slowly braised in soy sauce, and smoked bone marrow, with cubes of mushroom and a smooth purée around the beef. It is a Japanese ingredient cooked with a French sensibility, and the combination worked.", {"photo": "rowon5_11.jpeg"}, "A pale glazed tart dotted with white blossoms and clear beads of gel marked the transition toward the sweeter end of the menu, in the kitchen's typical style: restrained, pretty and exact.", {"photo": "rowon5_12.jpeg"}, "The first dessert is deliberately not sweet. In Miso & Truffle, the umami of Japanese miso is taken apart and rebuilt, and served with black truffle and ice cream, under a crown of shaved truffle. Salt and sweet change hands on the tongue, which prepares you for the desserts that follow. It is the most daring course on the menu, and the most memorable.", {"photo": "rowon5_13.jpeg"}, "Row on 5 pairs ambitious sourcing with a confident, playful kitchen, and it is highly rated for good reason. The trip ended happily, and I could not have asked for a better last meal in Europe."]},
  {"name": "Anglo Thai", "city": "London, United Kingdom", "stars": 1, "date": "2026-07-24", "keepOrder": true, "cover": "anglothai_cover.jpg", "body": ["AngloThai was the second restaurant of the day and the Thai counterpart to lunch: a one-star kitchen in London that cooks with Thai flavors and British produce. The printed menu is dated 24.07.26 and priced at £125, with a Signature pairing at £95 and an Austrian pairing at £125. Its British ingredients are a proud list: Brixham crab, Exmoor caviar, Cornish red chicken, Chalk Stream trout and Creedy Carver duck.", {"photo": "anglothai_1.jpeg"}, "The idea is clear from the menu: Thai dishes, rebuilt with local produce. Each line names a Thai element beside a British one, such as mee krob noodles with mussel, panang curry with sunflower seeds, and khao mok grains with biryani masala.", {"photo": "anglothai_2.jpeg"}, "The first bite was a crisp nest of mee krob noodles, topped with a poached piece of red chicken, glossy with sauce, and a tile of candied beetroot. It was pretty and playful, but the elements sat next to each other without quite becoming one.", {"photo": "anglothai_3.jpeg"}, "The Brixham crab in a small bowl of crushed ice, topped with Exmoor caviar and fennel fronds, came with coconut ash crackers cut in the shape of flowers and dotted with blue gel. It is elegant, and it is another dish that looks more complete than it tastes.", {"photo": "anglothai_4.jpeg"}, "A crisp-fried piece under a heap of fresh herbs and glistening caviar was the most abundant plate, and also the one where the herbs seemed to compete with, rather than support, what was underneath.", {"photo": "anglothai_5.jpeg"}, "The Creedy Carver duck with peppercorn curry and broad beans was the best illustration of what the kitchen can do. The duck was beautifully pink and the curry sauce was deep and glossy, and it showed the cooking when the elements do come together.", "But my overall impression is that the spices and the ingredients mostly go their own way. It feels as if the idea came first and the produce was then found to fit it, so the dishes lose their wholeness, and the flavors, in turn, lose their true taste. I rate it not so good.", "The day left me with a lesson that I think applies well beyond restaurants. Creative writing and running a restaurant are the same job. You need to know some technique and be willing to use a gimmick, and you also need your own ideas and a central point. But in the end, everything has to rest on developing the ingredient itself. It has to taste good."]},
  {"name": "Pantagruel", "city": "Paris, France", "stars": 1, "date": "2024-08-13", "cover": "pantagruel_cover.jpg", "body": ["Pantagruel is a one-star restaurant in Paris, and it is in my first tier of restaurant choices. I went on August 13, 2024, two days after the Olympic Games closed in Paris, when the city was still full of visitors and the restaurant was working at its usual calm pace. The chef was courteous, and the cooking showed real seriousness.", {"photo": "pantagruel_1.jpeg"}, "The kitchen is visible from the room through an arched opening, under copper heat lamps, and the brigade in navy jackets and aprons works with its heads down and no noise. That quiet concentration came through in the food.", {"photo": "pantagruel_2.jpeg"}, "The wine service is personal. A sommelier brings each bottle to the table and pours it there, starting with a 2023 Clos Mirages, a pale gold wine from an independent grower, which opened the meal.", {"photo": "pantagruel_3.jpeg"}, "The cooking holds to French tradition, built on the produce of Brittany and the Mediterranean south, and it is open to Japanese ideas. Elements such as grated daikon, dashi and katsuobushi are brought in to wake up the palate, and they work as seasoning rather than as decoration. One early course was a long, folded piece of fish in a pale wrapper, scattered with sliced green olives, lemon zest, tiny pink leaves and pistachio, lying in a bright orange sauce on a speckled sand-colored plate.", {"photo": "pantagruel_4.jpeg"}, "A langoustine tail followed, grilled so that the shell was charred in places, under a glossy golden sauce, with a stack of fine crisp strips, purple and green leaves and a dark purée beside it. The plate was a ribbed white porcelain that echoed the segments of the shell.", {"photo": "pantagruel_5.jpeg"}, "The Abatilles sparkling water from Arcachon on the table is a small regional touch that fits the Atlantic side of the menu.", {"photo": "pantagruel_6.jpeg"}, "The second pour was Domaine Loberger's Imagine 2022, an Alsace Pinot Gris made with skin maceration, in a copper-pink color that is far from the usual white. The label shows organic and biodynamic certification. It had more grip than a white and more lift than a red, and it suited the shellfish.", {"photo": "pantagruel_7.jpeg"}, "This was the plate I would choose as the picture of the meal: red lobster, in thick sections, laid along a curve of dark sauce, opposite a round of orange carrot on thin green courgette slices, dotted with small beads and with yellow petals and tiny leaves. The colors are bold, the sauce is deep, and the lobster was handled with confidence.", {"photo": "pantagruel_8.jpeg"}, "Another crustacean course used the shell as the plate: a pink half-shell, with a brown crumble on one end and spots of yellow purée each topped with a small leaf, and a little rice underneath. It is rustic and a little untidy, and the flavors were concentrated.", {"photo": "pantagruel_9.jpeg"}, "The main course was sliced game or duck, cooked rare, with the skin side seared, set on a puddle of dark reduced jus with small dark morsels, and a single glazed length of courgette. It was a classical French dish, simple and well executed.", {"photo": "pantagruel_10.jpeg"}, "The red was a 2019 Luxe, Calme et Volupté by Stéphanie and Luc Baudet, deep and dark in the glass, poured by the same sommelier from the bottle at the table.", {"photo": "pantagruel_11.jpeg"}, "A final scene shows how personal the room is: a server finishing a dish at a tray stand beside the table, with a lidded black pot, silver sauce boats and a gold leaf wafer on a black dish, in a dining room with shelves of cracked, gold-seamed ceramic eggs and a small painting of a rooster.", "Pantagruel stays with tradition while borrowing from Japan with discipline, and the host's care shows from the kitchen to the wine. It is a good restaurant, and I enjoyed it very much."]},
  {"name": "Cotogna", "city": "San Francisco, CA", "date": "2025-02-21", "cover": "cotogna_cover.jpg", "body": ["Cotogna is the rustic Italian sibling of Quince in San Francisco, from the same chef, Michael Tusk, and it is built around a wood-fired hearth. I ate there on February 21, 2025, at the counter facing the kitchen, and ordered a salad and a crab pasta.", {"photo": "cotogna_1.jpeg"}, "The seat is the best part of the meal. The hearth sits behind the pass, with a brick back wall, a live fire and a grate over the embers, and the cooks work it in plain view. In front of them were trays of pomegranate seeds, citrus, herbs and sauces ready for plating. It is a good kitchen to watch, and it tells you what the restaurant wants to be: simple food, cooked over fire, with seasonal produce.", {"photo": "cotogna_2.jpeg"}, "The salad was the plainest dish I ordered and also the prettiest. Little gem leaves, pink radicchio, slices of watermelon radish, dill and small purple mustard greens were lightly dressed in a heaped bowl. The produce was fresh and the dressing was restrained, which is the right instinct. But it was a salad of mixed leaves and nothing more, and I could not point to anything in it that I would not find at a good market restaurant.", {"photo": "cotogna_3.jpeg"}, "The pasta was the main event: a nest of fresh egg tagliolini, tossed with picked crab meat, parsley and a little chili, with a whole piece of leg meat laid on top and a pool of orange shellfish sauce on the plate. The crab was generous and sweet, the noodles were cut thin and well cooked, and the sauce had a clear seafood depth. Where it fell short for me was balance. The sauce pooled at the bottom of the bowl instead of coating the noodles, and the dish asked for more acidity or heat to lift it. It was a good plate of crab pasta, and a long way from a great one.", "Cotogna made the right decisions on ingredients and on the setting, and the cooking was sound. It did not give me a dish I am still thinking about. I rated it average: a comfortable, well-run Italian restaurant that I would be happy to go back to, and one that I would not travel for."]},
  {"name": "San Ho Won", "city": "San Francisco, CA", "stars": 1, "date": "2021-11-26", "cover": "sanho_cover.jpg", "body": ["San Ho Won is Corey Lee's Korean barbecue restaurant in San Francisco, the casual sibling of his three-star Benu. I went on November 26, 2021, the day after Thanksgiving, when the city was still easing back into indoor dining after the long months of the pandemic, and a new, well-publicized restaurant from a chef of Lee's standing was one of the more anticipated tables in town. It has since earned a Michelin star, but at the time of my visit it was simply the new place everyone wanted to try.", "My first taste of San Ho Won came well before that visit. The restaurant was about to open when the pandemic hit in 2020, so Benu, Lee's flagship, turned to takeout as the first way to present the dishes San Ho Won was planning to feature. I ordered it and ate it at home, long before I could sit down at the counter. The package was careful: a printed menu with instructions and a glossary, and each component packed separately. There was a traditional beef short rib soup (galbitang) to be finished at home with rice cakes and dumplings, served with a hot mustard sauce, a soy sauce and sesame leaf condiment, and radish kimchi. There was a salad of albacore and stonecrop (dol-namul) to be dressed right before eating, a Korean rolled omelette with a rock seaweed (dolgim) sauce, and a chilled omija and Asian pear hwachae with a pear panna cotta underneath. The glossary explained each ingredient, from garae-tteok to the five-flavor omija berry, and the instructions were precise about temperature and timing. It was a thoughtful introduction to the kitchen's approach, and it made the dinner in November 2021 feel like a second chapter rather than a first meeting.", {"photo": "sanho_0.jpeg"}, "When I finally sat down in the dining room on November 26, the experience was of a different kind: a full meal built around the grill.", {"photo": "sanho_1.jpeg"}, "The table is set in a stripped-down Korean style: a pale wood counter, steel cups of barley tea poured from a big steel kettle, and small steel dishes of banchan, among them sweet braised beans with tiny dried anchovies and a pile of napa kimchi. A paper sleeve with a tiger print holds the chopsticks. The restraint is the point, and it announces a place that wants to be a restaurant first and a spectacle second.", {"photo": "sanho_2.jpeg"}, "The pan-fried dumpling came with a thin, lacy crust spread out from the fold like a wing, and a bowl of chive dressing to dip it in. The crust was the best part, crisp and delicate, and a dish made with real care in how it was cooked, although the filling inside was less memorable than its looks.", {"photo": "sanho_3.jpeg"}, "The steamed egg arrived in a heavy black stone pot, risen over the rim, with scallions and a dusting of seaweed on top. It was soft, hot and comforting, a familiar side dish done well and served in the right vessel, though not a revelation.", {"photo": "sanho_4.jpeg"}, "The meat came sliced on a wire rack on a wooden board, with a steel basket of lettuce, perilla and other fresh leaves for wrapping, and a row of sauces and pickles: a dark soy-based dip, sliced green chilies, a red chili paste and a green herb sauce. The beef was charred and well seasoned, and the service of the wraps was generous and well organized. This is the heart of the meal, and it is a polished version of what Korean barbecue is: you take a leaf, add the meat, a sauce and a pickle, and fold.", {"photo": "sanho_5.jpeg"}, "Dessert was soft serve in paper cups printed with the restaurant's red seal, one topped with chocolate and crunchy caramelized grains and the other with caramel sauce. It was a playful ending, and a good one.", "I rate San Ho Won average. The ingredients were good, the room was handsome and the service ran smoothly, but nothing on the table made me feel I was eating something I could not find at a good Korean barbecue restaurant, and a fine-dining pedigree does not by itself make a meal exciting. It is a pleasant, well-made dinner, and I enjoyed it, but I would not go out of my way to return."]},
  {"name": "Benu", "city": "San Francisco, CA", "stars": 3, "date": "2018-11-24", "cover": "benu_cover.jpg", "body": ["Benu is Corey Lee's restaurant in San Francisco's SoMa, a three-star kitchen built on Korean and Chinese memory and Californian ingredients. Lee came from The French Laundry, and you can feel that discipline here, but the plates answer to a different tradition. On a late November evening in 2018, I went to see how far a tasting menu could stray from its French roots and still hold together.", "It starts before you sit down. The courtyard is lit like a small stage: maples turned yellow for autumn, ivy on the concrete wall, and rows of dark glazed jars, the kind used for fermenting soy and kimchi. Nothing is served here, yet it sets the theme. This is a kitchen that cares about time, brine and patience.", {"photo": "benu_1.jpeg"}, "The first dish that stayed with me was a small bowl of pale green with a single egg-shaped morsel at its centre, a quail egg in the spirit of the Chinese thousand-year-old egg, floating in a bright green sauce. It is quiet and almost austere, and it makes the case for the meal in a single spoonful.", {"photo": "benu_2.jpeg"}, "Then came the abalone shell, its mother-of-pearl catching the light, holding something lacquered and glossy in a dark glaze, dusted with tiny yellow and green flowers and herbs. The shell is the plate, the plate is the idea, and the dish looks like jewelry.", {"photo": "benu_3.jpeg"}, "The most playful moment was the soup. A celadon lid sculpted into a dorsal fin gives away the joke before you lift it: this is Benu's reimagining of shark fin soup, without the shark. Under the lid, glassy threads sit on a rust-orange broth in a matching celadon bowl. It is a pointed bit of theatre about Chinese banquet tradition, and a thoughtful one.", {"photo": "benu_5.jpeg"}, "The Korean thread comes through most clearly in the brass bowls. A tabletop of bangjja-style dishes held kimchi, dressed greens, a white pickle and a deep red paste, all around slices of pink pork under cracked pepper, a banchan spread rethought as fine dining. Almost everything arrives on a hammered metal plate or in handmade ceramic, in a dim room of dark wood, so the tableware is as memorable as the food.", {"photo": "benu_4.jpeg"}, "What impressed me most about Benu was its restraint and point of view. It never shouts its Asian roots, and it never dilutes them to please a Michelin audience. In 2018 that felt confident, and three stars felt earned."]},
  {"name": "Range Life", "city": "Livermore, CA", "date": "2025-02-16", "cover": "rangelife_cover.jpg", "body": ["Range Life is a neighborhood restaurant in Livermore, in the wine country east of the Bay Area, with a seasonal, California-leaning menu cooked with care and served in a plain, comfortable room with candles and fresh flowers on the tables. I went on February 16, 2025, and ate a run of shared plates and mains. I rate it a little above average.", {"photo": "rangelife_1.jpeg"}, "The meal opened with a raw fish crudo: thin pink-edged slices of fish, which looked like yellowtail, laid in olive oil with wedges of pale citrus and a snow of something finely grated on top. It was fresh and bright, and a sensible way to begin. A tall glass of citrus juice with mint and lime sat beside it.", {"photo": "rangelife_2.jpeg"}, "The beef tartare was served on a thick slice of charred sourdough, with a green herb and pepper relish and a heap of crisp fried shallots on top. The shallots gave crunch and a sweet, fried note against the cold, tender beef, and the toast was dark and smoky. It was hearty and a little rustic.", {"photo": "rangelife_3.jpeg"}, "The roast chicken was the dish of the evening for me: a half bird with a deep golden, lacquered skin, juicy white meat, and a pool of pan juices, set on a bed of soft polenta flecked with herbs. It was simple, well seasoned and well cooked, the kind of dish that depends on technique.", {"photo": "rangelife_4.jpeg"}, "A bowl of clams came with sautéed greens, slices of apple, pickled yellow peppers and big chunks of toasted bread, in a light broth. The clams were tender, and the apple and pickled peppers added acidity, though the bread and greens made it a little heavy and the dish felt more like a salad of parts than a single idea.", {"photo": "rangelife_5.jpeg"}, "The last plate was a sliced rosy meat, which looked like duck, over white beans, with roasted rounds of squash and zucchini, halved fresh figs and a thick, pale green sauce. The figs and the sauce were generous, and the beans were creamy. It was a handsome plate with plenty going on, though the many elements did not all pull in the same direction.", "Range Life cooks honestly and well, and the roast chicken and tartare are worth ordering. Some of the plates were busier than they needed to be. That keeps it a little above average for me, but it is a pleasant place to eat in Livermore."]},
  {"name": "Mijin", "city": "Seoul, Korea", "recommended": true, "date": "2025-10-20", "cover": "mijin_cover.jpg", "body": ["Mijin (Gwanghwamun Mijin) is a buckwheat noodle house in central Seoul, and the sign above the door says it all: “원조 1954”, original since 1954. The shopfront is covered in proof of its standing, with a certificate naming it a Seoul Future Heritage site (December 31, 2014), a poster showing its first log-cabin shop in Cheongjin-dong, where it ran from 1987 to 2008, and a row of red Michelin stickers on the window. I went on October 20, 2025. Its Michelin listing is a Recommended one, the guide's mark for good cooking without a star.", {"photo": "mijin_1.jpeg"}, "The table I ordered was a full spread of cold buckwheat noodles. The main portion arrived on a red lacquered tray with a bamboo mat, two heaped nests of thin, grey-brown noodles. A second smaller black bowl held more of the noodles, a plain and dark-grained strand that looks like the real thing, with the speckled color that buckwheat flour gives. With it came a bowl of dipping broth with a pinch of grated radish, a bowl of cabbage kimchi sitting in its red juice, and a dish of thick yellow pickled radish slices that cut the richness.", {"photo": "mijin_2.jpeg"}, "Beside the noodles was a black platter of sliced boiled pork, with a fringe of seared edge on the fat, spicy seasoned radish strips on lettuce, and a basket of fresh lettuce, with a small bowl of red chili paste to dress it. A steel kettle, which I took to be the hot noodle water, sat at the end of the table. The proportions are the point of the meal: plain, slightly nutty noodles that are cold and clean, set against salty, fiery side dishes.", {"photo": "mijin_3.jpeg"}, "The best bite was the wrap: a ruffled lettuce leaf holding a thick slice of the pork, its edge browned, and a tangle of the red seasoned radish on top. The pork was soft and not heavy, and the radish added heat and crunch, and the lettuce held it all together. It is simple, a classic Korean pairing, and it worked.", "I would not call Mijin a fine-dining experience, and it was not meant to be. It is an old, honest noodle shop, and the food on this table was clean, unfussy and enjoyable."]},
  {"name": "Bicena", "city": "Seoul, Korea", "stars": 1, "date": "2017-11-22", "cover": "bicena_cover.jpg", "body": ["Bicena is a one-star Korean restaurant in Seoul, and I went for lunch on a November day in 2017. The Michelin Guide had only started covering Seoul a year earlier, with its first edition in late 2016, and the city's new stars were still being discovered by visitors and locals alike. I wanted a decent, honest taste of Korean cooking at that level, and Bicena offered that in a quiet, well-mannered room.", {"photo": "bicena_1.JPG"}, "The meal opened with a small bowl of thinly sliced raw white fish, set on a black placemat. A knot of dark seaweed, a slice of radish, shiso, a dab of wasabi and a few fine strips of colored pepper sat beside it. It was clean, cool and light, and the plate was arranged with a Japanese-leaning restraint that suits Korean raw fish.", {"photo": "bicena_2.JPG"}, "The second course was gentler still: pale rolls wrapped in a thin sheet and filled with crab, greens and a bright orange vegetable, in a creamy sauce, in a broad matte-black bowl. It looked more European than Korean, and it tasted soft and mild, a quiet course before the table filled with bowls.", {"photo": "bicena_3.JPG"}, "The center of the meal was a Korean set. A wooden tray carried a bowl of seaweed soup, a bowl of white rice, and a ring of small dishes of banchan, among them marinated salmon and dressed greens, along with squares of toasted laver. Two abalone, still in their shells, arrived on a deep red plate beside it. This is the home-style table of rice, soup and side dishes, brought up to the standard of a starred dining room, and the white tableware and warm wood of the room suited it.", {"photo": "bicena_4.JPG"}, "The pork was the dish I remember best: thick slices of poached pork belly with a crust of toasted spice on the fat, a sheet of dark seaweed and strips of red kimchi on a white plate, with a lettuce salad in the background. The meat was tender and clean, and the seaweed and kimchi gave the salt and the heat. It is the logic of bossam, rewritten with a lighter hand.", "I rate Bicena above average. It gave me a decent, honest taste of Korean food in a polished setting, with careful plating and good ingredients. It did not surprise me, and the cooking stayed on the safe side, but it did what a good one-star lunch in Seoul should do, and I left satisfied."]},
  {"name": "KOMU", "city": "Munich, Germany", "stars": 2, "date": "2026-05-22", "cover": "komu_cover.jpg", "body": ["KOMU is one of the newest names in Munich dining, and it earned two Michelin stars almost immediately after opening. Chef Kunz cooks a single eight-course menu, priced at €295, with a wine pairing at €155. I went on a late-May evening, at the height of Bavaria's white asparagus season, and it is one of the rare restaurants I would call a personal favorite.", {"photo": "komu_1.jpeg"}, "The meal opened with a small, glossy tartlet: a ruffled golden shell holding a sphere under a thin green gel, finished with pickled seeds and dill. It sets the tone for the evening, with precise, delicate cooking that leans toward acidity and freshness over richness.", {"photo": "komu_2.jpeg"}, "The menu is a handwritten card that lists only a main ingredient and one or two companions: Danube salmon with physalis and tomato, schrenckii with pea and rowanberry, white asparagus with beechnut and salt plum, pollack with strawberry and jalapeño, John Dory with pistachio and green olive, Jersey smoke with cedro and cress, lemon verbena with cocoa fruit and morel, and cassis with ayran and dill. Read together, it is a map of the season: regional fish, spring vegetables and sour fruit.", {"photo": "komu_3.jpeg"}, "A caviar snack arrived on a perforated porcelain plate, a pearl of dark roe on a pale cream with crisp puffed grains. It was a quiet, luxurious opening gesture.", {"photo": "komu_4.jpeg"}, "The Danube salmon showed how comfortably the kitchen works with produce. Seared fish sat under thin slices of golden and red tomato and a small salad of herbs and flowers, in a clear, lightly oiled broth.", {"photo": "komu_5.jpeg"}, "The schrenckii course was served beneath a cloud of white foam, with caviar crowned by tiny white blossoms and surrounded by sweet green peas. It was a very spring-like plate, cool and green, in which the caviar was the salt and the peas were the sweetness.", "Germany's obsession with white asparagus is something every visitor should taste at least once, and KOMU treats it with lightness and precision. The stalk was clean and cool, with a root-vegetable freshness and a faint taste of spring groundwater, all of it brought forward rather than masked. This was the course that finally explained to me why Spargelzeit is a seasonal ritual rather than just a menu item.", {"photo": "komu_9.jpeg"}, "The pollack with strawberry and jalapeño was the course I admired most for its daring. Strawberry beside a white fish sounds avant-garde, but it follows the Nordic habit of treating berries as a source of acidity. The creaminess of the fish was pushed outward by the sharp fruit and the green heat of the jalapeño, and the combination worked perfectly. Kunz is a master with fish, and this dish showed that he is also willing to take risks.", {"photo": "komu_10.jpeg"}, "The John Dory followed, golden and tender, served with green olives, broad beans, a fan of shaved shallots and a pale sauce. It is a calm, savory counterpoint to the acidity before it.", "What made me love the meal most was the wine pairing, which has a complete set of values and a clear narrative. It is not just a gesture towards local producers: it was my first systematic tasting of Central and Eastern European wines. The common thread is small estates, small yields, low intervention, and hand-harvesting to protect acidity and temperature, so that the wine and the dish become one. At its best, a pairing like this changes how you understand the dish it accompanies.", {"photo": "komu_6.jpeg"}, "A Királyudvar Tokaji Furmint Sec from 2021 brought a volcanic mineral edge.", {"photo": "komu_7.jpeg"}, "A Burg Ravensburg Löchle Grauburgunder GG, from an estate that has been making wine for some 800 years, was razor-sharp, with acidity that ran in a straight line.", {"photo": "komu_8.jpeg"}, "The Voladeros from the Montes de Málaga, a mountain wine with an oxidative note from the high slopes, followed. The sequence ended with a vermouth from the Black Forest. Each pour was a different small estate and a different approach, and together they made a coherent argument.", {"photo": "komu_11.jpeg"}, "The meal closed with a pretty stack of pink and pale green sweetness on a blue-grey stone plate, light and bright, as a spring meal should end. KOMU is ambitious without being loud: it pairs a distinctive kitchen with a drinks program that has real conviction, and it is a place I would go back to."]},
  {"name": "Sukiyabashi Jiro Roppongiten", "city": "Tokyo, Japan", "stars": 2, "date": "2015-01-11", "cover": "sukiyabashi_cover.jpg", "body": ["Sukiyabashi Jiro is the most famous name in sushi, thanks to Jiro Ono and the documentary Jiro Dreams of Sushi. His Ginza flagship is nearly impossible to book. The Roppongi Hills branch is run by his younger son, Takashi Ono, and holds two Michelin stars. I went on a January evening in 2015, curious to see what the name tastes like at a counter I could actually get into.", {"photo": "sukiyabashi_4.jpg"}, "The room is what you would expect: pale wood, a deep red lacquer counter, an indigo noren with the restaurant's name, and a small lit niche of sake cups. Nothing is decorative for its own sake. Everything points at the counter, where the sushi is made and served one piece at a time.", {"photo": "sukiyabashi_2.jpg"}, "The nigiri arrives on a dark green glazed plate with a small mound of pickled ginger. The fatty tuna was a good example of the house style, a glossy slice with fine knife cuts, brushed with soy and set on small, loosely packed rice. It is pure and simple, and everything depends on the fish and the rice.", {"photo": "sukiyabashi_3.jpg"}, "The ikura gunkan, a tall wall of crisp nori holding glistening salmon roe, was just as clean. The restraint is the point here: no flourishes, only tidy and dependable execution at a steady pace.", "I rate Sukiyabashi Jiro neutral. It is a real piece of sushi history, and sitting at the counter of one of the Ono family's restaurants is worth doing once. But the fame is larger than the experience, and Tokyo has more to offer, both from traditional Edomae counters that go deeper into aging and technique, and from newer creative sushi-ya with more personality. I'm glad I went, but I wouldn't rank it among the best meals I had in Tokyo.", {"photo": "sukiyabashi_1.jpg"}]},
  {"name": "Sushi Kashiba", "city": "Seattle, WA", "date": "2024-04-11", "cover": "kashiba_cover.jpg", "body": ["Sushi Kashiba is the Pike Place Market counter of Shiro Kashiba, who brought Edomae sushi to Seattle in the 1960s and is often credited with teaching the city to eat it. I went on April 11, 2024, and sat at the sushi bar for the omakase, the part of the restaurant that I think matters most. I rated it high, and it earns that on the strength of the fish and the discipline of the hand.", {"photo": "kashiba_1.jpeg"}, "The service is classic. You sit across from the itamae, tell them how hungry you are and what you do not eat, and then stop making decisions. The pace is set by the chef, not by the kitchen, and each piece is placed in front of you at the moment it should be eaten. After a few courses I stopped looking at the menu altogether.", {"photo": "kashiba_2.jpeg"}, "The meal opens with a few small plates before the nigiri begins: a lightly dressed scallop with salmon roe, sliced tuna with a dab of fresh wasabi, and a small bowl of something bright with grated daikon. They are modest on purpose. They clear the palate and tell you the kitchen cares about balance, not display.", {"photo": "kashiba_3.jpeg"}, "Then the nigiri arrives, usually in short flights on a dark ceramic plate. The pieces I remember best were the Pacific fish: a glossy slice of tuna cured in soy, a medium-fatty cut with just enough fat to coat the tongue, a rich, silky salmon, and a sweet raw shrimp with its fried head served alongside. That last detail is the kind of thing a good omakase does, using the whole animal and giving you the crisp, savory head as the second act.", {"photo": "kashiba_4.jpeg"}, "The rice is the real signature. Each mound is small and warm, loosely packed so it falls apart in the mouth, and seasoned with a vinegar that supports the fish without announcing itself. The fish is cut generously but not so large that it overwhelms the rice, and the brush of nikiri soy means you never need to dip. Wasabi is grated and placed under the fish, so nothing needs to be added at the table.", "Service was warm and unhurried. The chef talked through the fish when asked and otherwise let the sushi speak. For a counter in a busy market district, the room was calm, and it felt like a place that takes the craft seriously without making you feel watched.", "As an omakase, Sushi Kashiba is excellent: traditional, precise, and honest about the quality of its ingredients. It is not the cheapest seat in Seattle, and it is not trying to be. If you are in the city and want to understand what good Edomae sushi tastes like in the Pacific Northwest, this is the counter I would send you to."]},
  {"name": "Canlis", "city": "Seattle, WA", "date": "2022-10-11", "cover": "canlis_cover.jpg", "body": ["Canlis has been Seattle's formal dining room since 1950, run by the Canlis family on the Queen Anne hill above Lake Union. When I ate there on October 11, 2022, the restaurant had come back from the pandemic with a set multi-course tasting menu in place of the old à la carte list. As I understand the 2022 menu, it leaned on Pacific Northwest produce and seafood with Japanese touches, and it moved away from the steakhouse classics, such as the Canlis salad and Peter Canlis prawns, that the house was long known for.", {"photo": "canlis_1.jpg"}, "The main course was a thick rectangle of beef, seared hard on the outside and left a bright, finely marbled pink within, finished with flakes of salt. It sat on a matte black plate beside a halved onion, cooked down until its surface was glossy and nearly black, and topped with three small white chive blossoms. A dot of very dark sauce sat between them. The beef was tender and rich, as the marbling suggests, and the onion was a smart, sweet and bitter counterweight. The plate was striking and restrained. But it was also a small, familiar combination of beef, onion and reduction, and the sauce was too sparing to link the two. It was careful cooking without much surprise, and the price tag at Canlis raises the bar.", {"photo": "canlis_2.jpg"}, "Dessert was a scoop of pale, creamy ice cream, tucked between shards of dark, caramelized crisp, with macerated blueberries and red berries and a single blue borage flower, served in a hand-formed, gold-rimmed bowl. A tea service in a cast-iron pot followed. The contrast of cold cream, tart fruit and brittle caramel worked, and the presentation was elegant. The dessert was pleasant and nothing more, and I found it hard to remember a distinct flavor a few days later.", "Canlis is a beautiful room with polished service and a history that few American restaurants can match, and the two dishes I show reflect that polish. They also reflect why I rated it only a little bit average. Technique and plating were beyond reproach, but neither course delivered the depth of flavor or the sense of discovery that its reputation and price promise. It is an experience worth having once for the setting and the story, with food that I found good but not special."]},
  {"name": "Aerlume", "city": "Seattle, WA", "date": "2024-04-09", "cover": "aerlume_cover.jpg", "body": ["I visited Aerlume in Seattle on April 9, 2024, and had two plates: a grilled octopus to start and a beef main. Both were well executed, and neither was a surprise.", {"photo": "aerlume_1.jpeg"}, "The octopus was the better-looking plate and the more ambitious one. A single charred tentacle lay across a bed of roasted cauliflower in yellow, white and green, with crushed potatoes, small pieces of dark sausage, torn mint and cilantro, and a spoonful of orange pepper sauce at the edge. The grill had given the octopus a lacquered, caramelized skin, which is what you want, and the suckers were crisp at the edges. The combination of smoke, spice, sweet roasted vegetables and fresh herbs is a familiar and sound one. My reservation is that the plate felt assembled from well-known parts, and the sausage and sauce tended to compete with the octopus instead of supporting it.", {"photo": "aerlume_2.jpeg"}, "The main was a classic: a round cut of beef, glazed in a dark, glossy reduction, over soft, buttery mash, with grilled asparagus, a handful of peppery arugula and crisp fried shallots and mushrooms at the back of the plate. It is a dish that is easy to like and hard to fault. The sauce was rich, the potatoes were smooth, and the asparagus kept a little bite and a little char. But it is also the dish that I would expect from almost any dining room with white tablecloths, and nothing on the plate gave me a reason to remember it.", "Taken together, the two plates showed a kitchen that is competent and polished, with sound technique and tidy plating. They also showed a kitchen playing it safe. I rated Aerlume average: I would not steer anyone away from it, but I would not plan a trip around it."]},
  {"name": "Wa Shin", "city": "Boston, MA", "date": "2026-08-19", "cover": "washin_cover.jpg", "body": ["Wa Shin (和心) is an omakase sushi counter in Boston, and it was a series of firsts for me: my first omakase in Boston, and my first sushi bar run by a Chinese itamae. I went on August 19, 2026, expecting a compromise, and found instead a clean, classic Edomae style. It shows in the restraint of the rice, the discipline of the fish, and the pace. To my taste, the level is at or above the average in Tokyo.", {"photo": "washin_1.jpeg"}, "The meal opened at the counter with a plate of thinly sliced pale fish, laid in a loose fold with a green herb sauce and a few tiny pink flowers, on a rough, ash-glazed dish. The chopsticks are wrapped in a paper band with the restaurant's name in brush script. It was cool and delicate, a quiet start.", {"photo": "washin_2.jpeg"}, "Fish is shown whole before it is cut, and here the chef held up a flat, silvery fish on a bamboo tray and talked us through it. That small ritual gives the counter a sense of where the evening's fish comes from.", {"photo": "washin_3.jpeg"}, "The nigiri that followed showed the Edomae hand at its best. This red-skinned piece, which looked to me like kinmedai, had its skin lightly seared so that the surface turned glossy and fragrant, over a small, loosely packed mound of rice. The fat under the skin carried the flavor and the rice stayed out of the way.", {"photo": "washin_4.jpeg"}, "A whole shrimp was brought out on a bamboo strainer, antennae still spread, before it was prepared. Seeing the shrimp whole is part of the pleasure of an omakase, and it was a good sign of freshness.", {"photo": "washin_5.jpeg"}, "The tuna was the showpiece: three layers of deep red lean tuna, stacked and scored on the rice, with a small mound of caviar and a curl of green onion on top. The tuna was clean and rich, and the caviar added salt without overpowering it.", {"photo": "washin_6.jpeg"}, "Since I was there, I ordered the lobster nigiri, expecting Maine lobster, given where we were. The chef instead showed a whole spiny lobster, from Hokkaido, and told us the kitchen does not trust Maine lobster for sushi. The piece on the plate in front of me was sweet and tender, with the springy texture the Japanese lobster is known for.", {"photo": "washin_7.jpeg"}, "A bowl of shredded crab came dressed in an amber jelly, topped with caviar and tiny pink flowers, in a hand-painted dish with a crab drawn in blue. It was the most ornate plate of the evening, and the flavors were clean: sweet crab, a savory jelly and the salt of the roe.", {"photo": "washin_8.jpeg"}, "Because we were eating sushi in America, I chose a bottle that suited the place. Dassai Blue Type 35 is brewed in the Hudson Valley, with water from the Hudson Valley and American-grown Yamada Nishiki rice. I did not expect much, but the clean sweetness of the 35% polish surprised me, and it was delicious. Alongside it, I tried a summer mikan (natsumikan) citrus drink served in a small tulip glass.", "The dessert was melon. Ending a sushi meal with fruit is the traditional way, and melon is the one I never want. That is a quibble, and a faithful nod to the tradition.", "I came to Wa Shin without high expectations for sushi in Boston, and left impressed. The fish was handled with care, the rice was restrained, and the style is a true Edomae one. It is a counter I would return to."]},
  {"name": "Korea House", "city": "Seoul, Korea", "date": "2025-01-22", "cover": "koreahouse_cover.jpg", "body": ["Korea House (한국의집) has served Korean royal court cuisine since 1957, from a traditional hanok compound in Seoul's Namsan-gol area. It carries three Blue Ribbon marks and a Taste of Seoul banner at the door, and it is one of the city's long-standing places to see gungjung eumsik, the food of the old palace kitchens, served as a formal set meal. I visited on a winter evening in January 2025.", {"photo": "koreahouse_1.jpeg"}, "The restaurant's current menu is built around seasonal Korean ingredients, and the Country of Origin card on the table makes the point clearly. It maps each main ingredient to where it was grown or caught: Wando abalone, Tongyeong red scallop, mussel and conch, Andong pheasant, Gongju chestnut, Jeju winter radish and black pork, and fatsia shoot and balloon flower root from the mountains. The set menu that evening followed the same logic, with an appetizer course (acorn bugak, buckwheat bingtteok, braised red scallop, brown turban shell porridge), a main course, and a royal hot pot (sinseollo) as the centerpiece.", {"photo": "koreahouse_2.jpeg"}, "The standout of my photos was a small shellfish course, glazed in a savory brown sauce and covered with a thatch of fine, toasted seaweed. The shellfish was tender and clean, the sauce was deeply savory, and the seaweed gave it a mineral edge. It is the kind of dish where the origin card earns its place, because the seafood tasted of the southern coast.", {"photo": "koreahouse_3.jpeg"}, "The sinseollo arrived in its traditional brass pot, a chimney in the middle with a small flame beneath, so that it stayed warm throughout the course. Inside were a clear, gently flavored broth, thin ribbons of egg jidan in yellow and green, mushrooms including a pom-pom of lion's mane, and pieces of meat and fish cake. It is a dish of old palace ceremony, and the delicacy of the broth was the point. It was comforting rather than thrilling, and I would have liked a little more seasoning.", {"photo": "koreahouse_4.jpeg"}, "The beef was sliced thin and braised until dark and glossy, and was served with roasted garlic, scallion, shredded lettuce and a strip of pear dusted with chili. The meat was tender, with a sweet soy depth, and the pear and the fresh lettuce cut through the richness well. It was a simple plate, and a good one.", {"photo": "koreahouse_5.jpeg"}, "The meal ended with a tray of sweets and drinks, which was the most charming moment of the evening. Two bowls held a chilled sweet soup with a golden jujube-like piece and a glazed fruit over crushed jelly. A small plate carried a pumpkin-shaped sweet, a candied persimmon, a stuffed dried tomato, and a black dasik cookie stamped with the character 壽, meaning long life. A tiny skewer stood in a vase beside them. It was playful, seasonal and beautifully presented.", {"photo": "koreahouse_6.jpeg"}, "Overall, I rated Korea House above average. The setting, the presentation and the care for regional ingredients are strong, and the pacing felt formal and unhurried. The flavors are restrained, as royal cuisine often is, so anyone expecting bold, spicy Korean food should look elsewhere. For a calm evening that explains how Korean court cooking tastes and looks, it is a worthwhile reservation."]},
  {"name": "Da Nerbone", "city": "Florence, Italy", "date": "2023-11-03", "cover": "danerbone_cover.jpg", "body": ["Da Nerbone is a market stall on the ground floor of Florence's Mercato Centrale, and it has been feeding the market's workers and visitors since 1872. It is a stand-up counter, with no table service and not much of a menu: the draw is the Florentine street food of boiled beef and tripe, and the one sandwich everyone orders is the panino con lampredotto.", {"photo": "danerbone_1.jpeg"}, "Lampredotto is the fourth stomach of the cow, simmered slowly until tender and chopped to order. The cook piles it into a crusty roll and finishes it with salsa verde, a bright mix of parsley, garlic and olive oil. The bread comes out of the pot lightly soaked in broth if you ask for it bagnato, which is the traditional way.", {"photo": "danerbone_2.jpeg"}, "What I ate was a good version of it. The meat was soft and rich, with the gentle chew you want from tripe and none of the harsh gaminess, and the green sauce cut the fat with herbs and a little heat. The roll held up well, crisp outside and soft inside, though it was a messy sandwich to eat standing up in a crowd. That is part of the experience.", "I rated Da Nerbone above average. It is cheap, fast and unmistakably Florentine, and the lampredotto is a fair introduction to the dish for anyone willing to try it. The setting is loud and busy, and you will need to be comfortable eating offal, but it is a worthwhile stop at the market."]},
  {"name": "Restaurante Porrue", "city": "Bilbao, Spain", "date": "2022-09-29", "cover": "porrue_cover.jpg", "body": ["Bilbao sits in the Basque Country, a region that treats ingredients with almost religious care: line-caught fish, aged beef and vegetables from nearby farms, cooked simply over fire or dressed with modern technique. Restaurante Porrue works in that tradition, with a dining room that is dim and modern and a kitchen that does both a grill and more composed plates. I visited on September 29, 2022.", {"photo": "porrue_1.jpeg"}, "The meal started with a roasted cauliflower, browned until its surface looked like caramel and set on a smooth golden purée, with crisp pink tuile shards and a small white blossom on top. The cauliflower kept a tender center and a nutty char, and the purée gave it a sweet, creamy base. It was a vegetable dish that did not need meat to hold the table's attention.", {"photo": "porrue_2.jpeg"}, "The second plate was a study in mushrooms: seared slices of king oyster and other wild mushrooms, a soft egg underneath, chives, flaky salt and a nest of raw enoki on top. The mushrooms were browned well and tasted deeply of the forest, and the egg gave the plate a silky richness. The raw enoki added a clean, crunchy contrast.", {"photo": "porrue_3.jpeg"}, "The octopus was one of the most striking plates. The tentacles were lacquered a deep mahogany, with a golden sauce, bright microgreens, a red powder and little copper-colored pearls. The texture was tender without being soft, and the glaze was sweet and smoky. It is a dish that looks flashy but tastes grounded in the Spanish classic of octopus and paprika.", {"photo": "porrue_4.jpeg"}, "Next came the most playful dish: a roasted bone, split lengthwise, topped with diced raw fish dressed with shiso and bubbles of mustard, and laid over a bed of popcorn. The mix of marrow richness, cool fish and sharp mustard was a surprise, and the popcorn added a warm, sweet crunch. It is a pairing I would not have thought of, and it worked.", {"photo": "porrue_5.jpeg"}, "The main fish was a whole turbot, served in a pool of buttery sauce with parsley and a side of braised red cabbage. The flesh was firm and sweet, the sauce had a gentle acidity, and the sweet-sour cabbage balanced the richness. It was a classic and confident plate.", {"photo": "porrue_6.jpeg"}, "The meat course was the highlight: thick slices of aged beef cooked over charcoal, pink and glistening, with the fat rendered golden and a scatter of flaky salt. It was served on its own small grill so that it stayed warm. The beef was deeply savory, with the funky depth of long aging and a crisp, caramelized crust. It is the plate I would order again.", "I rated Porrue more than above average. The cooking is skilled and varied, with a good sense of balance between modern presentation and Basque tradition, and the beef and octopus in particular were memorable. For a first-time visitor to Bilbao who wants a serious meal with a creative edge, it is a strong choice."]},
  {"name": "Higashi-Azabu Saikoh", "city": "Tokyo, Japan", "date": "2024-02-22", "cover": "saikoh_cover.jpg", "body": ["Higashi-Azabu Saikoh (東麻布さいこう) is a small sushi counter in Tokyo's Minato ward, run by chef Jun Saito (齋藤淳). The name means roughly “the best,” and the room is a good fit for it: pale hinoki, warm walls and a slate-tiled corner where a charcoal brazier sits. It is an Edomae-style sushi restaurant, with seasonal small plates and grilled dishes before the nigiri. Saito is also known in Tokyo for being the least solemn sushi chef in town, and that is part of the pleasure here. I came on February 22, 2024, the night before a more formal sushi dinner, to calm my nerves with a less serious meal. It turned out to be one of the meals I enjoyed most.", {"photo": "saikoh_1.jpeg"}, "I started with a bottle of Banshu Ikkon junmai ginjo, a summer light nigori, which the counter brought out with a flourish. It was fresh and softly sweet with a little cloudiness, an easy match for the early plates.", {"photo": "saikoh_2.jpeg"}, "The kitchen's charcoal brazier is the center of the room. Saito lifted skewers of sea urchin over the coals while steam rose behind, a theatrical start that also tells you the kitchen is serious about heat.", {"photo": "saikoh_3.jpeg"}, "The sea urchin came glazed and lightly warmed, a golden mound that kept the sweet, creamy character of uni with a faint savory char outside. It was rich, and I wanted another.", {"photo": "saikoh_4.jpeg"}, "The grilled cutlassfish (tachiuo) was a highlight. The silvery skin was seared until crisp and bubbled, while the flesh underneath stayed white, tender and juicy. A mound of grated daikon with herbs and a wedge of citrus kept it bright. It is a plate that shows how much skill a simple grill can show.", {"photo": "saikoh_5.jpeg"}, "A clay pot arrived at the counter bubbling, carried over with two towels by the second chef. It was a warm, comforting course in the middle of the meal, and the kitchen clearly enjoyed the moment as much as the guests did.", {"photo": "saikoh_6.jpeg"}, "The nigiri showed the Edomae training. The kohada (gizzard shad) had a bright, silvery skin and a mild cure, with the vinegar-forward rice just warm enough to complement it. The rice is seasoned with enough red vinegar to give the grains an amber tint and a clear acidity, and it is well suited to cured fish.", {"photo": "saikoh_7.jpeg"}, "The kuruma prawn, with its red and white stripes, was sweet and just barely cooked, springy to the bite. It was a clean, elegant piece that matched the more playful tone of the evening.", "Over the years, I have come to feel that sushi is no longer really about Michelin stars or fame. It is about finding the chef who becomes a friend, the one who quietly remembers your taste and has what you most want ready before you sit down. Saikoh is that kind of place, with a chef who is serious about the fish and not at all serious about the performance. I rated it more than above average, for the quality of the cooking and for how good the evening felt.", {"photo": "saikoh_8.jpeg"}]},
  {"name": "Schwartz's Deli", "city": "Montreal, QC", "date": "2016-12-04", "cover": "schwartz_cover.jpg", "body": ["Schwartz's Montreal Hebrew Delicatessen has stood on Boulevard Saint-Laurent, the Main, since 1928, when Reuben Schwartz, a Romanian-Jewish immigrant, opened it. It is often called the oldest deli in Canada, and it has one subject: Montreal smoked meat. The brisket is cured with a house blend of herbs and spices for about ten days, smoked, and steamed to order, then cut by hand and piled onto rye with yellow mustard. The room is narrow and crowded, with a counter, tight tables shared with strangers, and a line down the sidewalk. Nobody comes for the décor.", {"photo": "schwartz_2.jpeg"}, "For me, this deli has a personal weight. It is the restaurant where I had my first date with my current wife, on December 4, 2016, on a cold Montreal winter day. Over two plates of smoked meat and a bowl of poutine, at a table close enough to bump elbows with the next party, we got to know each other. I have eaten at far fancier places since, but few meals matter more to me.", {"photo": "schwartz_1.jpeg"}, "The sandwich is the reason to come. The meat was dark red, peppery and tender, with a smoky edge and a layer of fat that melted into the slices, stacked high between two slices of plain rye that were there only to hold it together. A stripe of sharp yellow mustard was all it needed. The meat does the work here, and it was excellent: more savory and spiced than pastrami from New York, and softer than the corned beef I knew.", "Next to the sandwiches was a bowl of poutine, the other Quebec classic, with thick-cut fries, squeaky cheese curds and a brown gravy. It was good and comforting, with the fries holding their shape under the gravy, though it is a side to the main event and not the specialty of the house. The paper placemats, printed in French and English with the menu, add to the feeling that nothing about the place has been updated for show.", "I rated Schwartz's more than above average. The smoked meat is the real thing, the setting is unpretentious, and the history is easy to taste. It will not suit anyone who wants a quiet table or a quick seat, and the line can be long in winter. But for a classic Montreal meal, or a first date as unforgettable as mine, I would not go anywhere else."]},
  {"name": "Trattoria Dall'Oste", "city": "Florence, Italy", "date": "2023-11-02", "cover": "dalloste_cover.jpg", "body": ["Trattoria Dall'Oste is a steakhouse in the center of Florence, a few steps off the tourist streets, and I went on November 2, 2023 for the one thing the city is known for at the table: bistecca alla fiorentina. It is not a Michelin restaurant, and I did not expect it to be. I wanted a proper Florentine steak, and that is what I got.", {"photo": "dalloste_1.jpeg"}, "The front is easy to spot, a red sign over a red carpet, with a small plaque for a World's 101 Best Steak listing by the door. People were already standing outside reading the menu, which says something about how much steak traffic this part of town sees.", {"photo": "dalloste_2.jpeg"}, "Inside, the meat is on display in glass dry-aging cabinets, rows of T-bones with their bone and fat cap still on, each tagged. Choosing your cut from the cabinet is half the theater, and it makes it clear that the kitchen builds its pitch around the beef and not around the menu.", {"photo": "dalloste_3.jpeg"}, "The room is warm, with yellow plaster walls, dark red wood and white tablecloths, and the table was set simply with a hammered brass bowl of bread, a bottle of sparkling Tuscan water and a Barolo. A Piedmont red is not the obvious Florentine pairing, but with a thick steak it did the job: structured, dry and firm enough to stand up to the char.", {"photo": "dalloste_4.jpeg"}, "The steak arrived on a black stone slab, sliced off the bone, with coarse salt scattered over it. The crust was properly charred and the inside was a deep rare pink, which is how a fiorentina should be served. The flavor was beefy and clean, with the char and the fat doing most of the work, and that is the signature taste of the Florence style. A little flag in the meat said Black Angus, so this was an imported breed and not the local Chianina the dish is traditionally made from. That is worth knowing before you order.", "I liked it, and I would say it is a little above average. The cooking was right and the meat was good, but nothing in the meal surprised me, and the setting and the cabinet display carry some of the experience. It is a solid place to eat a Florentine steak without leaving the center, and a good pick if you want the classic. If you want to taste the best the city can do, I would look further."]},
  {"name": "Eylan", "city": "Menlo Park, CA", "recommended": true, "date": "2026-11-12", "cover": "eylan_cover.jpg", "body_ja": ["Eylanは、Menlo Parkにあるインド料理のレストランです。シェフのSrijith Gopinathanは、インド各地の料理にカリフォルニアらしい感性を重ねています。この店は、パラタのようにサクサクの生地にDungeness crabを詰め、フェンネル、ポピーシード、ミントの濃厚なソースを添えた一品で知られ、レモングラス香るガルフ産の海老、チリを塗ったセロリアックとパイナップルの串焼き、鯛の丸焼きなど、薪火のグリルで力強い炭の香りをまとわせ、ソースにもほのかな燻香を移しています。私が食べたのはコースで、これらの料理ではありませんが、考え方は同じでした。まず目を奪われるのは店内です。カラフルな編みのランプが大理石のテーブルの上に下がり、白黒のストライプの陶器の照明が赤土色の打ち放しの壁に映え、ざくろの実をつけたオリーブの枝が天井から垂れています。庭をそのまま店に持ち込んだような、温かく少し芝居がかった空間です。",{"photo":"eylan_1.jpeg"},"注文したのはSigdi menuで、一人98ドル、四つのパートからなり、ワインペアリングも選べます。Sigdiは北インドの家庭で冬に使う炭火の火鉢で、人はその周りで暖を取り、その上で料理もします。メニューの名前はそこから来ていて、Eylanのコースも確かに火で焼いた味がします。",{"photo":"eylan_2.jpeg"},"最初の一皿は屋台の軽食です。Puchkaa（pani puri、semolinaの丸い揚げ殻）に、green grape chaas（スパイスを効かせた冷たいヨーグルトドリンク）を壺から注いで食べます。横の銀縁の小鉢にはsevが入り、ピンクと緑の小さな点が飾られています。殻は噛むとすぐ割れ、ドリンクは冷たく酸味があり、ぶどうの甘みがあります。手で食べるための料理で、私もそうして食べました。",{"photo":"eylan_3.jpeg"},"続いてcold smoked ahi tuna。角切りにして白いソースの上に盛り、緑のオイルの点を円状に散らし、ピンクの花と揚げたカレーリーフをのせています。横のテラコッタの器には、葉の形をしたクリスプが立てられ、すくって食べます。決め手はカレーリーフです。揚げると、嗅いだ瞬間に南インドとわかる香りがして、以前のインド人のAirbnbのゲストが料理していた時と同じ匂いでした。生のマグロが、きれいなタルタルから、帰る家のある味になります。",{"photo":"eylan_4.jpeg"},"二皿目は、sigdiの力が出る皿です。duck gelafi kebabは、ハーブとスパイスをまとった鴨のケバブで、ビーツのピュレの上に立ち、下には緑のオイルが敷かれています。隣は地元産の羊のひき肉を詰めたピーマンで、薪火で皮に焼き色がつくまで焼かれ、きゅうりのライタの上にのっています。どちらもよく焼けていて、脂っこくありません。鴨と羊でこれをやるのは簡単ではありません。竹の蒸籠に入った薄いパンと緑のチャツネで、皿に残ったソースまで拭って食べました。",{"photo":"eylan_5.jpeg"},"メインはcharred black codとCornish hen tikkaから選べ、私はタラにしました。表面は黄金色にカリッと焼かれ、揚げたココナッツがのり、下にはchayote thoran、まわりには、ターメリックとココナッツのグレービーが敷かれ、カレーリーフと唐辛子を添えたご飯の小鉢が付いてきます。これはケーララの伝統的な食べ方で、以前のAirbnbのゲストの故郷の味です。皮はカリッと焼かれて出てきますが、味はちゃんと家の味で、ただ洗練されています。これは言うほど簡単なことではありません。",{"photo":"eylan_6.jpeg"},"デザートは二皿です。charred pineapple & jaggeryは、焦がしたパイナップルを太陽のように並べ、真ん中に炙ったインドのミルクケーキのアイスクリームをのせ、ライムの皮を散らし、シロップにはインドの有名なダークラムOld Monkを使っています。gajar halwaは北インドのにんじんのデザートで、普通は温かくて重いプディングですが、ここでは冷たい角切りにして、青りんごとディルのソースに置き、cashew chikkiとすみれの花を添えています。多くのインドの家庭が知っているデザートの、軽やかな解釈です。",{"photo":"eylan_7.jpeg"},{"photo":"eylan_8.jpeg"},"私はEylanを、今の水準のままで高く評価しています。インド各地の料理にカリフォルニアの感覚を加え、火で調理して、素敵な空間で出す。一人98ドル（別に20%のサービス料と任意の3%の従業員向け加算）という、はっきりした考えのある店です。心に残ったのは親しみやすさです。ケーララのタラは、ゲストが作ってくれた故郷の味に、より洗練が加わったものでしたし、マグロのカレーリーフは彼らのキッチンの匂いそのものでした。客に帰ってきたと感じさせるのは、技術だけで教えられることではありません。Eylanにはまだ上がる余地があるとも思います。考えはすでに自信に満ちていて、細部と仕上げをもう少し磨けば、今よりずっと大きな店になるはずです。また来ますし、これからも見守ります。"], "body_ko": ["Eylan은 Menlo Park에 있는 인도 레스토랑입니다. 셰프 Srijith Gopinathan은 인도 각 지역의 요리에 캘리포니아의 감각을 더합니다. 이 집은 파라타처럼 바삭한 반죽에 Dungeness crab을 채우고 펜넬, 양귀비씨, 민트로 만든 진한 소스를 곁들인 요리로 알려져 있고, 레몬그라스 향의 걸프 새우, 칠리를 바른 셀러리악과 파인애플 꼬치, 통 도미 같은 요리에는 장작불 그릴로 강한 숯불 향을 입히며 소스에도 은은한 훈연 향을 더합니다. 제가 먹은 것은 코스여서 이 요리들은 아니었지만, 접근 방식은 같았습니다. 먼저 눈에 들어오는 것은 공간입니다. 알록달록한 짠 조명이 대리석 테이블 위에 걸려 있고, 흑백 줄무늬 도자기 조명이 붉은 흙빛 노출 콘크리트 벽에 어우러지고, 석류가 달린 올리브 가지가 천장에서 늘어져 있습니다. 정원을 그대로 식당으로 옮겨 놓은 듯 따뜻하고 약간 연극적입니다.",{"photo":"eylan_1.jpeg"},"저는 Sigdi menu를 주문했고, 1인 98달러에 네 파트로 구성되며 와인 페어링은 선택 사항입니다. Sigdi는 북인도 사람들이 겨울에 쓰는 숯 화로로, 둘러앉아 몸을 녹이고 그 위에서 요리도 합니다. 메뉴 이름이 여기서 왔고, Eylan의 코스도 정말 불로 구운 맛이 납니다.",{"photo":"eylan_2.jpeg"},"첫 코스는 길거리 간식입니다. Puchkaa(pani puri, 속이 빈 세몰리나 튀김)에 green grape chaas(향신료를 넣은 차가운 요거트 음료)를 주전자에서 따라 먹고, 은색 테두리의 작은 그릇에는 바삭한 sev가 분홍색과 초록색 점으로 장식되어 있습니다. 튀김은 입에 넣자마자 부서지고, 음료는 차갑고 새콤하며 포도의 단맛이 납니다. 손으로 먹도록 만든 음식이고, 저도 그렇게 먹었습니다.",{"photo":"eylan_3.jpeg"},"다음은 cold smoked ahi tuna입니다. 참치를 깍둑썰기해 흰 소스 위에 쌓고, 초록색 오일 방울을 동그랗게 두르고, 분홍 꽃과 튀긴 커리 잎을 올렸습니다. 옆의 테라코타 그릇에는 잎 모양 크리스프가 세워져 있어 떠먹을 수 있습니다. 핵심은 커리 잎입니다. 튀기면 맡자마자 남인도라는 걸 아는 향이 나고, 예전 인도 에어비앤비 손님들이 요리할 때 나던 냄새와 같았습니다. 정갈한 타르타르였던 생선이 돌아갈 집이 있는 맛이 됩니다.",{"photo":"eylan_4.jpeg"},"두 번째 코스에서 sigdi가 힘을 발휘합니다. duck gelafi kebab은 허브와 향신료를 입힌 오리 케밥으로, 비트 퓨레 위에 서 있고 아래에는 초록색 오일이 깔려 있습니다. 옆에는 이 지역 양고기 다짐육을 채운 고추가 장작불에 껍질이 부풀 때까지 구워져 오이 라이타 위에 놓여 있습니다. 둘 다 잘 구워졌고 느끼하지 않습니다. 오리와 양고기로 이렇게 하기는 쉽지 않습니다. 대나무 찜기에 담긴 얇은 빵과 초록색 처트니로 접시에 남은 소스까지 닦아 먹었습니다.",{"photo":"eylan_5.jpeg"},"메인은 charred black cod와 Cornish hen tikka 중에서 고를 수 있고, 저는 생선을 골랐습니다. 겉은 바삭하게 구워 튀긴 코코넛을 올렸고, 아래에는 chayote thoran, 둘레에는 강황과 코코넛 그레이비가 깔려 있고, 커리 잎과 말린 고추를 얹은 밥이 작은 그릇에 따로 나옵니다. 이것은 케랄라의 전통 방식이고, 예전 에어비앤비 손님들의 고향 맛입니다. 접시에 나온 생선은 겉이 바삭하게 구워져 있지만 맛은 여전히 집의 맛이고, 다만 더 세련됐습니다. 말처럼 쉬운 일이 아닙니다.",{"photo":"eylan_6.jpeg"},"디저트는 두 접시입니다. charred pineapple & jaggery는 그을린 파인애플 조각을 해처럼 둘러놓고 가운데에 살짝 토치한 인도 밀크 케이크 아이스크림을 올리고 라임 껍질을 뿌렸으며, 시럽에는 인도의 유명한 다크 럼 Old Monk를 썼습니다. gajar halwa는 북인도의 당근 디저트로 보통 따뜻하고 묵직한 푸딩인데, 여기서는 차갑고 깔끔한 직사각형으로 만들어 청사과와 딜 소스 위에 놓고 cashew chikki와 제비꽃을 곁들였습니다. 많은 인도 가정이 아는 디저트를 가볍게 풀어낸 해석입니다.",{"photo":"eylan_7.jpeg"},{"photo":"eylan_8.jpeg"},"저는 Eylan을 지금 수준 그대로 높이 평가합니다. 인도 각 지역의 요리에 캘리포니아의 감각을 더해 불로 조리하고 멋진 공간에서 내놓는, 생각이 분명한 식당입니다. 1인 98달러(별도로 20% 서비스 요금과 선택 사항인 3% 직원 추가 요금)입니다. 마음을 끈 것은 친근함이었습니다. 케랄라식 대구는 손님들이 해주던 고향 음식에 세련됨을 더한 맛이었고, 참치 위의 커리 잎은 그들 주방의 냄새 그대로였습니다. 손님이 집에 온 것 같다고 느끼게 하는 것은 기술만으로 가르칠 수 없습니다. 저는 Eylan이 더 올라갈 여지가 있다고도 생각합니다. 아이디어는 이미 자신감이 있고, 디테일과 마무리를 조금만 더 다듬으면 지금보다 훨씬 큰 식당이 될 것입니다. 또 올 것이고, 계속 지켜보겠습니다."], "body_zh": ["Eylan是Menlo Park的一家印度餐厅，主厨是Srijith Gopinathan，他把印度各地的风味和加州的做法融在了一起。这家店以一款酥脆、类似印度抛饼的蟹肉馅饼闻名，里面是Dungeness蟹，配茴香、罂粟籽和薄荷调的浓郁酱汁；明火炭烤的香茅海虾、辣椒釉芹菜根和菠萝烤串、整条海鲷，也都带着扎实的炭香，连酱汁里都有淡淡的烟熏味。这次我吃的是tasting menu，没点这些菜，但思路是一致的。一进门先被餐厅的环境吸引：彩色编织灯罩挂在大理石餐桌上方，黑白条纹的陶灯映在锈红色的清水混凝土墙上，橄榄枝缀着石榴从天花板垂下来。像把花园搬进了餐厅，温暖，又带一点戏剧感。",{"photo":"eylan_1.jpeg"},"我点的是Sigdi menu，每人98美元，分四个部分，另有可选的配酒。Sigdi是北印度人冬天用的炭盆，围着它取暖，也在上面做饭。菜单以此命名，Eylan的tasting menu也确实是火烤出来的味道。",{"photo":"eylan_2.jpeg"},"第一道是一组街边小吃。Puchkaa（就是pani puri，中空的粗麦脆球）配一壶green grape chaas，一种加了香料的冰镇酸奶饮料，倒进脆球里吃；旁边银边小碗里是脆脆的sev，点缀着粉色和绿色的酱点。脆球一咬就碎，饮料清凉微酸，带着葡萄的甜味，整道菜就是要用手抓着吃。",{"photo":"eylan_3.jpeg"},"接着是冷熏黄鳍金枪鱼，手切成丁，堆在白色酱汁上，周围一圈绿色油点，再放一朵粉色小花和几片炸咖喱叶，旁边的陶罐里立着一片叶子形状的脆片，用来蘸着吃。关键在咖喱叶：炸过之后那个香味一闻就知道是南印度的，和之前印度房客做饭时一样。它让这道生鱼从一道精致的鞑靼，变成了有“家”的味道。",{"photo":"eylan_4.jpeg"},"第二道是火的主场。鸭肉卷（duck gelafi kebab）裹着香草和香料，立在甜菜泥上，下面是一圈绿色的油；旁边是塞了本地羊肉末的青椒，明火烤得起泡，铺在黄瓜酸奶酱上。两样都烤得很好，不腻，鸭和羊肉要做到这样并不容易。竹蒸笼里的薄饼和绿色酸辣酱，正好把盘底剩下的酱汁蘸干净。",{"photo":"eylan_5.jpeg"},"主菜可以在炭烤黑鳕鱼和Cornish hen tikka之间选一个，我选了鱼。鱼皮煎得酥脆，上面铺着炸椰丝，下面是chayote thoran，盘里是姜黄椰奶咖喱，旁边小碗里的米饭放了咖喱叶和干辣椒。这是喀拉拉的传统吃法，也是之前Airbnb房客家乡的味道。端出来是一块煎得很脆的鱼，味道还是家里的，只是更精致了，这比听上去要难得多。",{"photo":"eylan_6.jpeg"},"甜点有两道。炭烧菠萝配jaggery（棕糖）：焦糖化的菠萝块围成一个太阳，中间是烤过的印度奶糕冰淇淋，撒了青柠皮，糖浆里用了印度有名的Old Monk黑朗姆。Gajar halwa是北印度的胡萝卜甜品，通常是热热的、很浓稠的布丁，这里做成了清爽的冷切块，放在青苹果和莳萝酱上，配腰果chikki和一朵紫罗兰。是对大多数印度家庭都熟悉的甜品的一次轻松改写。",{"photo":"eylan_7.jpeg"},{"photo":"eylan_8.jpeg"},"我对Eylan的评价很高，就是它现在这个水平。它想法清楚：印度各地的菜，加州的风格，用火烤出来，在一间好看的餐厅里，每人98美元（另加20%服务费和可选的3%员工附加费）。最打动我的是它的亲切感：喀拉拉的鳕鱼像是房客做给我吃的家乡菜，只是更精致；金枪鱼上的咖喱叶，一闻就是他们厨房的味道。能让客人有回家的感觉，这不是技术能教出来的。我也觉得Eylan还有上升的空间：想法已经很自信了，细节和收尾再打磨一点，会比现在大得多。我还会再来，也会一直关注它。"], "body": ["Eylan is an Indian restaurant in Menlo Park, and the cooking is that of chef Srijith Gopinathan, who blends regional Indian cuisine with a Californian sensibility. The kitchen is known for a flaky, paratha-like flatbread filled with Dungeness crab, served with a rich sauce of fennel, poppy seeds and mint, and for wood-fired grills that give lemongrass-scented Gulf shrimp, chili-glazed celeriac and pineapple kebabs and whole sea bream a robust char, while adding a subtle smoke to the sauces. I came for the set menu rather than those dishes, but the philosophy was the same on my table. The room is the first thing you notice. Woven lanterns in orange, teal and ochre hang over a pale marble table, striped ceramic pendants glow against a wall of rust-colored board-formed concrete, and olive branches heavy with pomegranates trail down from the ceiling. It is an Indian dining room dressed as a garden, warm and a little theatrical, and it sets an expectation of generosity that the kitchen mostly meets.",{"photo":"eylan_1.jpeg"},"I ordered the Sigdi menu, $98 per person, a four-part tasting menu with an optional wine pairing. A sigdi is the charcoal brazier North Indian households use in winter, a pan of embers that people sit around for warmth and cook on. The menu is named for it, and the cooking lives up to the name: the smoke and char come from real fire, not from a garnish.",{"photo":"eylan_2.jpeg"},"The first course arrives as a set of street-food snacks. Puchkaa, the hollow semolina shells better known as pani puri, come with a small pot of green grape chaas, a spiced and chilled yogurt drink poured into the shell by hand, and a silver-rimmed bowl of crisp sev dotted with pink and green. The shells shatter on contact, the drink is cool and sour with a sweetness from the grapes, and the whole thing is built to be eaten with your fingers, which is how I ate it.",{"photo":"eylan_3.jpeg"},"Cold smoked ahi tuna followed, hand-cut and heaped on a pool of white sauce ringed with dots of green oil, finished with a pink blossom and a few fried curry leaves. A leaf-shaped crisp stands in a terracotta pot beside it for scooping. The curry leaves are the detail that counts. Fried, they give off an aroma that is unmistakably South Indian, the same smell that used to come out of the kitchen when my Indian Airbnb guests cooked, and it lifts the raw fish from a careful tartare into something with a home address.",{"photo":"eylan_4.jpeg"},"The second course is where the sigdi shows. A duck gelafi kebab, a minced-duck kebab wrapped in its own coat of herbs and spice, stands on a beet purée over a slick of green oil. Next to it, a long pepper stuffed with local lamb kheema is blistered over wood fire and laid on cucumber raita. Both are properly grilled and neither is greasy, which is not easy to manage with duck and lamb. Thin rounds of flatbread and a bright green chutney arrive in a bamboo steamer for mopping up whatever is left on the plates.",{"photo":"eylan_5.jpeg"},"For the main course there is a choice between charred black cod and Cornish hen tikka. I took the cod. It came with a crackling, deeply browned crust of fried coconut, set on a chayote thoran in a pool of turmeric and coconut gravy, with a bowl of rice flecked with curry leaf and dried chile alongside. This is a Keralan way of cooking, and it was the dish my former Airbnb guests from Kerala would have recognized from their mothers' kitchens. The fish was seared until crisp on the outside and tender within, and the flavor was that of a home kitchen, only more refined, which is a rarer thing than it sounds.",{"photo":"eylan_6.jpeg"},"Dessert was two plates. Charred pineapple and jaggery came as caramelized wedges arranged like a sun around a torched meringue-like dome of Indian milk cake ice cream, with lime zest and a syrup made with Old Monk, India's famous dark rum. The gajar halwa, the carrot dessert of North India that is usually served as a hot, heavy pudding, was reinvented as a clean, cold block on a green apple and dill sauce with cashew chikki and a violet. It is a cheerful play on a dish most Indian families know by heart.",{"photo":"eylan_7.jpeg"},{"photo":"eylan_8.jpeg"},"I rate Eylan highly, and I mean at the level it is at today. It is a restaurant with a clear idea, regional Indian cooking with a California accent, done over fire in a handsome room, for $98 before the 20% service charge and the optional 3% employee surcharge. What won me over was how personal it felt. The Kerala cod tasted like the food my Airbnb guests used to cook, only more refined, and the curry leaves on the tuna made the whole dish smell like their kitchen. A restaurant that can make a guest feel at home like that is doing something that technique alone cannot teach. I also think Eylan has room to grow. The ideas are already confident, and a little more polish in the details and the finish would make it a much bigger restaurant than it is now. I will be back, and I will be watching where it goes."]}
];

